	pages = {311 - 325},
	doi = {10.1002/9781394250424.ch21},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105026004103?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {The adoption of personalization by news media has profound implications for news reception. By individualizing news delivery, personalization can help individuals comprehend and interpret information about societal developments. However, it can also limit the diversity of individual information diets and amplify news avoidance. To account for the complex relationship between personalization and news reception, we provide an overview of the extant research on the topic, focusing on three key areas: the perceived impacts of personalization on news reception, empirical assessments of personalization effects, and normative implications of these effects. We conclude by discussing the importance of contextualizing the research on the impact of news personalization depending on different types of personalization systems and forms of journalism. We also outline several directions for future research, including extending it beyond the current focus on the Global North and examining the impact of personalization on a broad range of aspects of news reception. © 2026 by John Wiley & Sons, Inc.},
