	doi = {10.1109/ICTCT69201.2025.00037},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105037448582?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Recommendation systems are now essential for helping users sort through the vast amount of digital information. News recommendation is particularly challenging because articles are continuously produced and quickly become outdated. Traditional methods like K-Means clustering, content-based filtering, and bandit algorithms such as Thompson Sampling [2] and Upper Confidence Bound (UCB) [2] are effective but struggle to find the right balance between personalization and exploring new content. In this research, we introduce a hybrid solution that combines UCB and Thompson Sampling to achieve a better balance. Using the Microsoft News Dataset (MIND), our experiments show that the hybrid model outperforms the individual methods in terms of accuracy, diversity, and user engagement. To strengthen our findings, we include statistical significance testing, confidence interval analysis, and robustness checks to confirm that the improvements are both significant and meaningful. We also place our research within the context of recent developments in 2024-2025 related to transformer-based and fairness-aware recommender systems. Overall, these contributions indicate that our hybrid model not only performs well but also consistently provides personalized and engaging news recommendations. © 2025 IEEE.},
