	abstract = {News recommendation systems aim to personalize content by aligning it with user preferences. However, this personalization often leads to the formation of filter bubbles that reinforce existing biases and limit exposure to diverse perspectives. While existing studies have primarily focused on a single dimension of bias, this paper investigates the dynamics of filter bubbles across multiple dimensions, including news topic, sentiment, and political leaning. Real-world news often blends these dimensions, and focusing on only one risks missing critical patterns in how personalization shapes user exposure. Our findings reveal a complex interplay: a decline in diversity along one dimension (e.g., topic) often intensifies biases in others (e.g., sentiment or political leaning), reinforcing multi-dimensional filter bubbles and further narrowing exposure. Moreover, advanced contextual understanding in modern news recommendation models accelerates this compounding effect, amplifying filter bubble formation across all dimensions over time. These results underscore the importance of analyzing these dimensions jointly and highlight the challenge of balancing personalization with content diversity. Additionally, we contribute a valuable resource for future research, including a dataset of annotated news articles and a simulated news recommendation environment to study the formation and evolution of filter bubbles. Both are available at https://github.com/annresearch/NRSim. © 2025 IEEE.},
