	abstract = {The accelerated growth of digital content demands the guaranteed information delivery on the basis of individualism and accountability in the news recommendation systems. One of the contributions of the paper is a proposal of an independent, AI-based system that would allow curating a personalized newsstream, thereby reducing the strengthening of bias and enhancing diversity, as well as user engagement. The framework consists of Sentence-BERT (SBERT) to achieve better semantic understanding, Determinantal Point Processes (DPP) to select diversities, and a Deep Q-Network (DQN) to control the decision-making behavior of reinforcement. SBERT embeddings facilitate a finer content/user profile fit by finding out the contextual nuances that go beyond keyword matching. The informed reordering by DPP adds not only topical but also ideological variety, so that redundancy is lessened and filter bubbles are counteracted. The DQN agent learns independently on how to balance user satisfaction with fairness with a longterm reward policy that punishes bias reinforcement. Empirical findings prove that the implemented framework provides better personalization accuracy, intra-list similarity decrease, and steady policy convergence compared to traditional ranking models. The system provides a base to ethical, adaptive, and scalable artificial intelligence agents in news personalization through the amalgamation of semantic modeling, probabilistic diversity, and reinforcement learning. The study will help in advancing viable and transparent recommender systems, which will be able to create knowledgeable consumption as well as democratic media values. © 2025 IEEE.},
