	abstract = {Recently public opinion is mostly shaped or modified by the media and digital social platforms. The presence of increasing media bias deviates the users from the original and objective information. This fosters a skewed perception, underscoring the importance of transparency in media. Addressing this, we propose a system that detects and mitigates media bias using Natural Language Processing (NLP) and Machine Learning (ML) techniques. Our ensemble model for analysing media bias to provide fair and transparent news journalism (FairJEn), combining RoBERTa, Bi-LSTM, with XGBoost meta-classifier, achieved superior performance with 77.5% accuracy and a Macro Average F1 score of 0.705. To mitigate identified biases, we employed Pointwise Mutual Information (PMI) for bias identification and BERT-based contextual replacements for content refinement. This approach maintained high semantic similarity score of 0.9997 with minimal semantic shift of 0.0047. This ensures the integrity of the original content. A news recommendation system leveraging Siamese Networks was developed to diversify the content exposure. Transitioning from cluster-based recommendations to a dataset-wide approach improved bias coverage and diversity, with the score of 0.6 and 1.52 respectively. Our proposed ensemble technique for bias detection and mitigation enhances the usage of media consumption with balanced content delivery. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer-Verlag London Ltd., part of Springer Nature 2026.},
